  "question": {
    "text": "The electron of a hydrogen atom makes a transition from the $(n+1)^{\\text {th }}$ orbit to the $n^{\\text {th }}$ orbit. For large $\\mathrm{n}$ the wavelength of the emitted radiation is proportional to",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "3IRLM",
      "text": "\n$n$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "CZwBL",
      "text": "\n$n^3$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "2xYEw",
      "text": "\n$n^4$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "FBQRG",
      "text": "\n$n^2$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "If $n_1=n$ and $n_2=n+1$ Maximum wavelength $\\lambda_{\\max }=\\frac{n^2\\left(n+1^2\\right)}{(2 n+1 R)}$\nTherefore, for large $\\mathrm{n}, \\lambda_{\\max } \\propto n^3$",
    "image": null
  },
